<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/paysandu" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paysandu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-2">2 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Brazilian and Colorado forces capture Paysandú in Uruguay</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Brazilian troops and the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> Party took the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paysandu">Paysandú</a> from its Uruguayan Army defenders, ending a siege begun on 3 December 1864. The Uruguayan War set the governing Blanco Party against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and the Colorado Party.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paysandu">Paysandú</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-31">31 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">US House passes the Thirteenth Amendment abolishing slavery</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">After intense lobbying by President Lincoln, the House of Representatives approved the amendment by 119 votes to 56. Ratified in December 1865, it ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-1">1 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Lincoln signs the resolution for the Thirteenth Amendment</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> added his signature to the congressional resolution sending the amendment to abolish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> to the states, though presidents play no formal role in amendments. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> ratified it that day.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-8">8 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Delaware rejects the Thirteenth Amendment</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Delaware's legislature voted against the amendment abolishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, although the state had remained in the Union. It ratified the amendment only symbolically, in 1901.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-20">20 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">A peace accord at the Villa de la Unión ends the Uruguayan War</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Venancio Flores and Manuel Herrera y Obes, for the government of Tomás Villalba, signed a peace accord at the Villa de la Unión. A general amnesty was granted to Blancos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorados</a>, and Villalba handed the presidency to Flores.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-1">1 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Sheridan breaks the Confederate line at Five Forks</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Union troops under Philip Sheridan routed Confederates at Five Forks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, cutting Lee's last supply railway into Petersburg. Lee abandoned Petersburg and Richmond the next day.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-2">2 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Jefferson Davis abandons Richmond as Petersburg falls</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">As Union troops broke through the Petersburg lines, Confederate President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jefferson-davis">Jefferson Davis</a> and his government fled Richmond by train. Lee surrendered at Appomattox a week later.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-4">4 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Lincoln walks through the fallen Confederate capital</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a> toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richmond-virginia">Richmond, Virginia</a>, two days after its evacuation, greeted by crowds of newly freed Black residents. He sat briefly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jefferson-davis">Jefferson Davis</a>'s desk in the Confederate White House.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-9">9 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Lee surrenders to Grant at Appomattox Court House</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a> surrendered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-northern-virginia">Army of Northern Virginia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a>. Other Confederate forces followed in the weeks after, bringing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> to an end.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/theatre" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theatre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-14">14 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">John Wilkes Booth shoots Lincoln at Ford's Theatre</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Five days after Lee's surrender, the actor John Wilkes Booth shot President Lincoln during a play in Washington. Lincoln died the next morning, the first US president to be assassinated.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/theatre" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theatre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-15">15 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Abraham Lincoln dies of his wound</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Lincoln died at 7.22 am in a boarding house opposite Ford's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a>. Andrew Johnson was sworn in as president hours later, inheriting the task of Reconstruction.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/boston" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boston.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-26">26 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">John Wilkes Booth is cornered and killed in Virginia</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Union troops trapped Lincoln's assassin in a tobacco barn and set it alight; Booth was shot by Sergeant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> Corbett and died within hours. It ended a twelve-day manhunt.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-10">10 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Union cavalry capture Jefferson Davis in Georgia</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The fleeing Confederate president was seized near Irwinville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. He was held for two years at Fort Monroe but never brought to trial.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-19">19 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Union troops proclaim emancipation in Galveston, Texas</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">General Gordon Granger announced that all enslaved people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> were free, two and a half years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emancipation-proclamation">Emancipation Proclamation</a>. The day is celebrated as Juneteenth, a US federal holiday since 2021.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/london" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-5">5 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">William Booth begins the mission that becomes the Salvation Army</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Methodist preacher William Booth began evangelising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>'s East End, founding the Christian Mission. Renamed the Salvation Army in 1878, it grew into a worldwide church and charity.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-14">14 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Edward Whymper's party makes the first ascent of the Matterhorn</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A seven-man team led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-whymper">Edward Whymper</a> reached the summit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matterhorn">Matterhorn</a>. On the descent a rope broke and four men fell to their deaths, a tragedy that shocked Victorian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Shaw University is founded in Raleigh, North Carolina</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Shaw University began as a theological class of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> in the Guion Hotel in Raleigh, North Carolina; it is the oldest historically Black university to begin offering courses in the South. It took its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elijah">Elijah</a> Shaw in 1870.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-6">6 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Georgia ratifies the Thirteenth Amendment, abolishing slavery</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> ratified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution</a>, which made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> illegal except as punishment for a crime; that day it reached the required 27 of the then 36 states. It was the first of the three Reconstruction Amendments.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconstruction-era">Reconstruction</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-18">18 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The 13th Amendment abolishing slavery is proclaimed</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Secretary of State William Seward certified that the 13th Amendment had been ratified by the required number of states. It abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, freeing about four million people.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-triple-alliance" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-triple-alliance.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-triple-alliance">Treaty of the Triple Alliance</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Treaty of the Triple Alliance was signed in secret on 1 May 1865 in Buenos Aires by Argentina, Brazil and Uruguay after Paraguay invaded Argentine territory.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-appomattox-court-house" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-appomattox-court-house.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-appomattox-court-house">Battle of Appomattox Court House</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Appomattox Court House, fought in Virginia on 9 April 1865, was the last engagement of Robert E. Lee's Army of Northern Virginia.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/morant-bay-rebellion" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/morant-bay-rebellion.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Revolt</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/morant-bay-rebellion">Morant Bay rebellion</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Morant Bay rebellion was an uprising of poor black farmers in Saint Thomas Parish, Jamaica, in October 1865, led by the Baptist deacon Paul Bogle.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/assassination-of-abraham-lincoln">Assassination of Abraham Lincoln</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The assassination of Abraham Lincoln took place on 14 April 1865, five days after the surrender of the main Confederate army, when the actor and Confederate sympathiser John Wilkes Booth shot the president at Ford's Theatre in Washington; Lincoln died the…</p></div></li></ol></section>
